Title:
Multi-Robot Active Perception: Toward Autonomous Sports Videography and More!

Abstract:

Aerial robots (drones) are becoming increasingly integrated into our society,
industry, and media.
Prominently, drones are being used to carry sensors and cameras to film,
observe, and inspect the world around us.
As this goes on, we can expect to see autonomy continue to improve and greater
numbers of drones to be involved in more varied and complex tasks.

For autonomous drones, processes of navigating and observing unknown objects and
environments are pervasive across mapping, tracking, inspection, and
cinematography tasks.
Often, performing these tasks involves reasoning about what a robot or team of
robots will observe and quantifying the utility of the robots' actions.

During this talk, I will focus on development of methods for coordinating robots
for videography tasks such as for filming team sports.
Today, products from companies such as Skydio and DJI enable individual robots
to autonomously film and track a moving athlete.
Yet, it is not obvious how to coordinate a team of robots to film a group of
moving people.

Consider the example of a square dance.
Groups are constantly splitting, joining, and rearranging!
These phenomena pose challenges for coordination based on formations or
assignments.
Instead, we suggest directly optimizing robot views and motions.
Our approach incorporates two main contributions:
1) design of view rewards that encourage even distribution of pixels over the
scene and surfaces of people being filmed and 2) methods for coordinating
multiple robots and views based on greedy planning and submodular optimization.
As a product of the view planning process, we are essentially able to solve for
assignments and formations implicitly, and I will show examples of these
behaviors from our simulation results.

Finally, in NAPPLab (the Navigation, Aerial-robots, and Perception Planning
Laboratory) we are actively working on projects involving autonomous
videography, robot inspection and exploration, and perception-aware
navigation and control.
